## Migration: `TimedExecutor` → .NET 8

### Pattern Classification

Your code is a **timeout enforcement** pattern with a **ThreadAbortException catch + ResetAbort** inside. Three APIs must go:

| Removed API | Why it breaks on .NET 8 |
|---|---|
| `thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Never thrown — dead code |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var token = cts.Token;

        Task<T> task = Task.Run(() => work(), token);

        try
        {
            // Task.WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException on timeout
            return task.WaitAsync(token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
        }
    }
}
```

### Key Changes

1. **`CancellationTokenSource(timeout)`** replaces `Thread.Abort()` — auto-cancels after the deadline.
2. **`Task.Run`** replaces manual `Thread` creation — uses the thread pool.
3. **`Task.WaitAsync(token)`** (.NET 6+) replaces `thread.Join(timeout)` — throws `OperationCanceledException` when the token fires.
4. **`catch (OperationCanceledException)`** replaces `catch (ThreadAbortException)` — the modern equivalent.
5. **`using var cts`** ensures the `CancellationTokenSource` timer is disposed.

### Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Work doesn't actually stop** | Cooperative cancellation only works if `work()` checks the token. If `work` is a tight CPU loop or blocking I/O that ignores cancellation, the task continues running in the background even after you get the `TimeoutException`. |
| **Passing the token to `work`** | If the work can cooperate, change the signature to `Func<CancellationToken, T>` so it can check `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` at checkpoints. |
| **Uncooperative code** | If `work` calls third-party code you can't modify, move it to a **child process** and use `Process.Kill()` — that's the only safe forced termination in modern .NET. |

### If work needs to cooperate (recommended)

```csharp
public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
    var token = cts.Token;

    Task<T> task = Task.Run(() => work(token), token);

    try
    {
        return task.WaitAsync(token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        throw new TimeoutException(
            $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
    }
}

// Caller example:
executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(token =>
{
    foreach (var item in items)
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint
        Process(item);
    }
    return result;
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
```

This ensures the work **actually stops** when the timeout fires, rather than silently continuing on a thread-pool thread.
